WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
Rockin' Ball!

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:04.620
Pinball is famous in video game history!

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:27.560
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.020
I'm Mike. I'm Sean, who's currently on fire.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.700
And I'm Joe.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:44.520
Sorry, our listeners are gonna hear a pretty hardcore siren right now, so I thought I'd play into that a bit.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.880
Yeah, I don't know how I said so confidently that you weren't on fire.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.580
For all we know, you are the fire.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:54.880
Yeah, honestly, like the fire department is not coming now because you told them he's not on fire.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.640
Yeah, you gotta be careful there.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.120
It's like a pinball machine.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.860
Haha. Great segue, Sean, because like the back of the box states, this is pinball.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:13.740
Um, whether pinball is famous in video game history, I guess is really debatable, especially this early on for something in the back of the box.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:14.700
Like, I don't know.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.620
I don't think of pinball machines as video games.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.140
They're pinball machines.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:34.580
And thus, if they're saying that it's famous in video game history, they're saying that like, video game pinball is like one of the most popular genres of video game genres is that like everybody always makes pinball video games.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.980
Like, Super Mario Brothers Pinball, everybody loves that.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:46.860
Yeah, no, like, pinball machine is a, is a strictly analog device, uh, with physical objects.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.700
And it might have like a video readout and like some animations that play.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:57.760
But that's like, that's just a little accessory to the, it's closer to a board game.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:04.860
Sean, what do you say to the people who, um, now emulate, uh, pinball machines using strictly like digital solutions?

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:14.360
Then, like, you're using a, you're playing a video game based on a board game, like, uh, like, uh, like a card game, you know?

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.540
Right, like playing Texas Hold'em. The game of life on PS1, yeah.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.260
Texas Hold'em's not a video game.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.080
But there are video game versions of it, yeah.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:29.480
Right, and so this is a video game version of pinball, but this is not what I think of as like the famous part of video games.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:34.580
Yeah, back of the box is kind of like, it's kind of like video game pinball.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:35.940
The thing you love.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.440
And it's like, well, wait a minute.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.160
You don't tell me what I love.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:45.260
The last time we saw pinball on this console was at the very beginning, right?

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.020
The original black box pinball game that Nintendo released.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.180
Since then, nobody has decided to put out, like, another option for pinball on this console.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.080
And I liked that.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.660
I liked the original video pinball.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:06.720
I mean, Mike, you remember back in our college days, we'd play a lot of video game pinball, and it brought me back to that.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.840
It's simple, but it, I enjoy it.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:18.300
But if I remember correctly, that game was a lot more, like, strictly pinball.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:27.660
Where, I think that maybe we mentioned that in the episode, that, like, unless I'm totally misremembering, but that, like, being a video game that could have done some more things with it.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:36.020
Which I think that this game, I'm sure we'll get into, does do a few more things that, like, you can't do, or it would be harder to do with a real pinball machine.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:38.940
Joe, you are a true nostalgia historian.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.640
We did say that in the episode.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:46.680
And we are definitely going to get into Rock and Ball's many different twists on pinball.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:56.040
Because while the game does have a regular pinball mode that is, like, 50s themed, or rock and roll themed, or diner themed, I couldn't figure out what they were going for.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.760
Because there's, like, cheeseburgers, and classic cars, and music notes and stuff.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:06.880
So I'm not sure if it was just, like, 1950s in general, or rock and roll that they were going for.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:08.180
Generic pinball.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:11.900
Yeah, there's a TV, there's a shoe, there's a jacket, there's a burger.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:13.720
There's many other things, too.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.220
But, like, it's generic pinball at its finest.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.720
That is a, you know, one-player to four-player experience.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:22.720
And you don't need four controllers.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.720
You just hand it off to the next person when your turn's over and you compete for the high scores.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.300
But more interestingly, you have, like, characters to be.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:35.840
It's not enough for you to clearly be the person hitting the buttons that are the flippers.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.500
You have to pick the character that has no other personality.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.280
Just a name, essentially, and a sprite for them.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.920
And then you'll never see them until you get to the score screen again.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:51.460
But they are the person that you are playing the pinball machine as.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:58.980
It just feels like a complicated step in pinball because I never really thought I was playing as somebody else while I'm playing pinball.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:06.200
But I guess, you know, if you want to be Tom or Billy or John or Paul, Lisa and Diana are all in this game.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:14.040
Yeah. I think it would have made more sense to, you get into, like, a character creator like The Sims.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.860
And you create two characters.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:23.820
And you would be playing the character that would sort of combine the DNA of those two people.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:32.660
So that you're creating the parents of the character that you end up playing the pinball with your hands with.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.780
Yeah, that's if you really wanted to simplify it, though.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:40.500
Yeah. But I also think that, like, yeah, it is way too simple.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:49.680
Like, I'm always for, I'm all for giving the player some sort of, like, agency in this, like, immersion at the beginning.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:55.360
But, like, I feel like you gotta go a little farther than, like, pick a character and you'll see their face at the end of the game again.

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:57.440
Give them a stat or something.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:00.500
Put their face on the bottom of the screen, make it look like they're doing something.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:02.520
Joe, remember Casino Kid?

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:04.000
I do. Great game.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:14.460
Love it. In that one, you face off against a lot of other, like, actual characters who have distinct personalities and tells and levels of skill.

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:21.140
That feels like what they should have done with this pinball character mode for everybody other than, like, who you are.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:26.840
Like, it would be a mistake to say you picked Tom and now you're better at flippers, right?

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:29.520
But Billy gives the ball more speed, right?

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:30.580
Like, that would be the mistake.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:38.040
Like, but if you're gonna verse these people, right, just selecting them, like, go through, you know, Tom is really good at saving the ball, right?

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:39.140
Like, he's more on it.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.400
But Billy's great at knowing, like, how to do the trick shots.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:48.200
Like, that's fine because they're computer players that you're choosing to play against for specific scores or whatever.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:51.240
It's really just difficulty modes, if I'm being honest, as I'm describing it.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:59.200
But I think they were smart not to give these people any actual stats, which is weird because I would say the opposite in almost any other kind of game.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:05.080
But here, since it's just, like, an additional piece of flavor, it's not something that we should prolong much longer.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:13.860
But just to be clear, the characters do have, like, a victory dance and they do say something when they win or if they lose.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.880
So there is a little something extra.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:17.460
There is a reason for them to exist.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:22.480
It's not just an additional screen that's a chore before you finally get to play pinball.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:24.000
But it's not enough for me.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:29.360
I'd say that you're giving it more credit than it's due, but I'll allow it.

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:32.400
So the regular pinball, let's start there.

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:40.340
We mentioned the weird theme around, like, you know, things that kids in the 80s probably don't give a shit about.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:41.540
We're in 90s now.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.680
But the 1990s is really, like, still the 80s, right?

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:46.220
It's the 1990 itself.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:47.920
I mean, you know, where you draw the line.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:54.080
Yeah, exactly. Like, I think, like, you gotta, it takes, like, five years before you get to the new genre.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:55.300
Yeah, we've gotten to this. Okay.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.760
So this is just pinball themed around what their parents would be interested in.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:01.940
But, hey, go for it.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:13.380
The rules are the same as all other pinball machines except for one very, like, I don't know, just weird rule that you can unlimited tilt in this game.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:15.000
You can tilt as much as you want.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.140
And that is not true in real pinball machines.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.340
They do allow for a light tilt if you can, you know, be sensitive enough.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:32.220
But any pinball machine worth their salt is going to tell you that it's going to say tilt and all of a sudden the flippers are going to lock and your ball is just going to go out.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.660
And you're going to be caught cheating.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.140
In this game, they're encouraging the cheating.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:38.780
And, like, I get it.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:44.920
I appreciate that there's a little bit more to this than just the flippers.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:50.880
I think maybe giving it, like, you get a couple tilts.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:56.960
Like, if you wanted to go for a closer analog for the real life pinball experience.

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:06.380
But I kind of like it as more of, like, we're adding this feature to pinball so that there's more interaction.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.340
And it's not just left and right.

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:13.740
Yeah, I mean, I liked it, too, as someone who's really never played any serious pinball.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:18.160
Like, I mean, I've played a pinball machine in my life, but, like, I really don't know much about it.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:20.000
So that didn't even come across my mind.

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:22.020
Like, oh, how come I can do this unlimitedly?

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:24.360
I just assumed, like, oh, yeah, that's normal.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:30.120
I think if they branded this game a little differently, like, as if it were, it was, like, pinball for rebels.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:37.200
And this was, like, the MLB, not MLB, NFL Blitz.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:42.200
Yeah, NFL Blitz version of pinball for, like, hardcore kids.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:44.440
Like, that would help sell it a bit.

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:52.700
In my first three minutes of playing regular pinball, I was using the tilt function because, you know, I read the manual beforehand.

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:55.140
I knew that I was allowed to tilt as much as possible.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:10:02.880
And it was great because it was it makes it, like, easier to get your ball stuck in those bumpers and then just constantly racking up points in them.

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:10.860
What I didn't expect is that on the lower half of the screen, there is, like, two on the right-hand side.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:14.480
There's, like, two bumpers that score some good amount of points.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:17.040
But, you know, they're kind of, like, in an odd position.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:18.600
You're not expected to hit them or whatever.

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:30.400
Well, using the tilt as it was coming off the edge, my ball got wedged between both of those bumpers and was stuck there permanently, just racking up an infinite score.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:36.700
Interestingly, the game can only count a certain amount on the screen.

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:43.780
But when you do go to the, like, put in your high score after you run out of all three balls, it reveals the much higher score.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:47.360
I was surprised how far it was able to count up.

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:49.600
So how long did you leave it on?

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:52.100
Maybe, like, four minutes.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:59.160
Okay. I wanted to test the limits because I wanted to see if it had, like, a kill screen or something.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:02.580
Yeah, yeah. Arcade games kind of have those, so why wouldn't a pinball machine?

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:06.860
But the pinball machine only counts to, like, 999,999.

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:13.380
The high score machine theoretically could count up to 999 million, I believe.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:22.540
Wow. It's interesting that it doesn't have the digits on, like, the regular screen, but it is, like, internally keeping track of it and, like, still knows how to display that.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:24.000
Right. Like, is it distracting?

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:36.040
I don't know. Otherwise, though, this is just regular pinball, and, you know, I have a lot of thoughts about pinball as a, you know, as a form of play.

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:42.960
But I don't think I want to talk about the placement of the ramps or the bumpers or, like...

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:53.220
Well, yeah, this pinball, this standard pinball, like, you know, with most well-designed pinball machines, there are usually, like, several different kinds of, like, objectives.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:54.800
At least in the newer...

00:11:54.800 --> 00:12:09.680
You probably know, like, what the era of pinball would be, but from what I remember from our little forays into it, like, there's a couple things that, like, oh, do this X times, and then it'll open up this ramp or whatever.

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:32.400
Whereas in this, there's, like, one thing like that, and you have to knock down these, like, temporary bumper things, and once you do that, you have access to this other thing, and when you hit that, it opens up a door to, like, a bonus stage, and you can spend pretty much however long you can survive in this bonus stage.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:36.340
But, like, that's really the only, like, bell and whistle on this board.

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:43.940
Yeah, and we're well ahead of all of that, you know, what you're referencing of missions or objectives and stuff like that.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:49.800
That was... that was even as far back as, like, the early 70s, really, the idea of that.

00:12:49.900 --> 00:13:00.420
Before that, it was really just, you know, there was a nine-ball pinball machine that looked like pool, and you had to hit all the pool balls, and that would, you know, trigger something.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:03.800
But it was still, like, a simple just hit the bumpers, ring up some points.

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:11.580
But by the 70s and definitely the 80s, you know, you have games that want you to, like, you know, they're doing some storytelling.

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:40.100
Yeah. So, yeah, it does make for a rather uninteresting regular pinball game because you are just scoring for points when there are other modes that make pinball more complicated, right?

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:50.320
So this is maybe scratching the itch for somebody who doesn't want to spend unlimited quarters at the arcade playing real pinball and realizing that they're not nearly as good as they thought, which is a thing.

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:58.960
You know, you could be good at video game pinball and go to a pinball, a real pinball machine, and realize you just don't have the same, like...

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:05.220
I don't know if it's cognition or whatever, but the flippers behave differently in real life for sure than they ever do in a video game.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:10.580
Yeah, one thing that I wished that you could do in this is sort of have, like, a light touch on the flipper.

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:12.520
But... Yeah, you can't do that here.

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:15.760
Yeah, I thought maybe, like, if you hold it, you do a hard touch.

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:21.200
If you hold it for, like, a half a second, and if you literally tap it for, like, a millisecond, it would be a light touch.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:22.320
That would have been nice.

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:29.260
I don't know technically how possible that is, but I feel like we've had things like that with jumps, character jumps and stuff in games.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:30.480
And that would have made...

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:33.280
That would have given me, like, a little more...

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:40.920
Another tool in my arsenal to do something other than, like, trying to hit it when it's, like, at a certain distance away from my flipper or something.

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:42.260
It gives you a little more agency.

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:52.960
The one thing you can do with the flippers that is definitely a beginner skill in pinball is you can use them to hold the ball in the place.

00:14:53.320 --> 00:15:01.740
And then, so this way, you can release it and figure out exactly when you want to hit the ball with the flipper to hit exact spots.

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:03.820
So, it's good that that's possible.

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:04.840
It's not that hard to do.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:09.200
I don't even know if they would have to program that just based on the size of the flippers themselves.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:19.000
But that's a great way to hit things that you want to instead of just constantly having the ball come down, hitting the flippers, and hoping it hits something.

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:23.100
Right. So, moving on from regular pinball, we have...

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:27.480
I actually don't remember what the mode's called, but it's, like, a gambling form of pinball.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:29.440
Is it, like, the nine ball thing?

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:30.360
Yeah, the nine ball.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:40.720
Yeah, balls are knocked into, like, a pachinko pattern where beforehand there's a bunch of holes on the board, on the pachinko board, and you stop.

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:46.340
You press the A button to stop these numbers that are floating around on the holes, and there's just ones and twos.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:58.220
But, basically, when you stop it, those numbers become locked, and then you want to get your ball in those holes in order to win your bet.

00:15:58.580 --> 00:16:05.480
So, you bet, like, you know, a hundred bucks or whatever based on the ones you see, and then you hope that you get yours into, like...

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:07.980
If you get it into a two, you get double your payout.

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:10.540
If you get it into three ones, you get...

00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:12.380
That would be triple your payout.

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:15.120
So, you know, it's just a mode like that.

00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:18.160
What do we think about the pachinko aspect?

00:16:18.340 --> 00:16:19.440
Is this even pinball?

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:21.820
Well, there's...

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:26.220
Okay, so... Also, if you get them in, like, a line, like a...

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:32.120
Like an XO thing, like, you can also score points that way.

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:33.360
It counts that as something.

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:40.200
But this is a mode that can take a very long time, because there are a lot of...

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:45.220
Obstacles that this ball can hit, and it could be a very...

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:55.360
It could take a while for it to even hit, like, the bottom of the screen where your flippers are for you to, like, shove it back up there and take its time again.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:04.780
But... And, like, as the game goes on, as you get balls and more holes, those are now other places where the ball can't go.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.140
So, it's...

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:08.540
It can take a while, and...

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:09.620
Um...

00:17:09.620 --> 00:17:10.860
It can also...

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:18.660
Get into weird spots where it'll just bounce, like, perfectly on top of a thing over and over.

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:22.720
So... I found this one kind of frustrating and not too fun.

00:17:23.080 --> 00:17:30.860
See, I would think this one was a little more fun if this, like, gambling element had a little more purpose.

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:32.420
Like, you get these credits.

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:34.760
It's fun in a vacuum.

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:47.900
Well, not in a vacuum, but it's fun to think about, like, oh, okay, like, I can bet however many of these credits I want, and then I can, like, get double what I bet based on, like, if I get it into this hole or if I, you know, get a straight line here or whatever.

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:52.080
I can, you know, build that up, but there's no purpose to these credits, right?

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:54.940
I mean, you just bet more credits on your next game if you want.

00:17:55.120 --> 00:18:03.740
Like, I don't know what exactly I would want them to do, but I feel like it would be cool to be able to spend them in other modes or, like, I don't know.

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:07.580
Right. The credits should be what you have to play the other modes.

